      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 2009, Docaviv started an annual documentary film festival in Ma'alot‑Tarshiha to bring high‑quality cultural activity to the Israeli periphery.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ma'alot-Tarshiha",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "A 4th‑century burial cave in Tarshiha yielded a cross and a glass piece engraved with a menorah, hinting at early Christian and Jewish presence.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ma'alot-Tarshiha",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The city’s name derives from Hebrew “Teir” meaning fortress and Arabic “Shīḥ” meaning fragrant herb, reflecting its layered history.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ma'alot-Tarshiha",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Tarshiha was once a major cotton‑producing village and administrative center of the Galilee’s nahiya during the Ottoman era.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ma'alot-Tarshiha",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "In 1266, Crusader troops raided Tarshiha, an event noted in medieval chronicles.",
